{"name":"GHK-Cu skin protocol","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":59,"casual":{"lead":["GHK-Cu is a copper-binding tripeptide found in human plasma and used in cosmetics under the name copper tripeptide-1. Skin protocols built on it aim for firmer, smoother skin, better wound and post-procedure healing, and in some cases hair.","Lab studies show it stimulates collagen and glycosaminoglycan production in fibroblasts, and small human trials report anti-wrinkle and post-laser effects. Reviews note that data on skin penetration and effectiveness are limited."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["The commonly discussed route is topical serums and creams, sometimes with microneedling; some peptide communities also discuss injectable forms. Results are described over weeks to months."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Topical products are generally cosmetic; injected or self-compounded forms are outside approved use, with sterility and quality concerns discussed in community threads. Evidence in humans is limited."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Redox chemistry of copper-amyloid-beta: the generation of hydroxyl radical in the presence of ascorbate is linked to redox-potentials and aggregation state.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17577900/","source":"Chembiochem : a European journal of chemical biology","year":2007,"cites":204,"kind":null},{"title":"The human tri-peptide GHK and tissue remodeling.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18644225/","source":"Journal of biomaterials science. Polymer edition","year":2008,"cites":124,"kind":"Review"},{"title":"Stimulation of collagen synthesis in fibroblast cultures by the tripeptide-copper complex glycyl-L-histidyl-L-lysine-Cu2+.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3169264/","source":"FEBS letters","year":1988,"cites":118,"kind":null},{"title":"Regenerative and Protective Actions of the GHK-Cu Peptide in the Light of the New Gene Data.","url":"https://pubmed.n