 acne scars","url":"https://looksmax.org/threads/will-ghk-cu-2-mg-help-with-my-hypertrophic-acne-scars.1768636/","site":"looksmax.org","archived":"https://web.archive.org/web/20251217223905/https://looksmax.org/threads/will-ghk-cu-2-mg-help-with-my-hypertrophic-acne-scars.1768636/"}],"news":[],"social":[],"web":[{"title":"Copper peptide GHK-Cu - Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_peptide_GHK-Cu"}]},"angle":{"goal":"Skin","chain":["GHK-Cu applied to skin","Fibroblast collagen and GAG synthesis","Firmer, smoother skin and faster healing"],"effect":"claimed","text":"Users apply it to improve fine lines, texture and recovery after resurfacing or microneedling."},"wikipedia":null,"grokipedia":null,"lead":["The GHK-Cu skin protocol refers to routines built around the peptide GHK-Cu (glycyl-L-histidyl-L-lysine bound to copper) for skin and hair appearance. It is used topically in anti-aging products, and in peptide communities some discuss injection or microneedling with it.[1][2]","Loren Pickart isolated it from human plasma albumin in 1973, and it was identified as a tripeptide in 1977.[3] Cell studies show collagen and glycosaminoglycan stimulation.[4] Levels in plasma reportedly decline from about 200 ng/ml at age 20 to about 80 ng/ml by age 60.[5]","A randomised trial of copper-tripeptide skin care after CO2 laser resurfacing was published in 2006.[6] A 2025 review concluded cell data support GHK as an anti-wrinkle ingredient but that skin permeability and effectiveness data are insufficient.[1]"],"infobox":[["Compound","GHK-Cu (copper tripeptide-1)"],["Discovered","1973, Loren Pickart[3]"],["Cosmetic use","Anti-aging, reparative[3]"],["Routes discussed","Topical; injection in peptide circles"]],"sections":[{"h":"Mechanism","paras":["In cell studies GHK-Cu stimulated collagen synthesis in fibroblast cultures and increased glycosaminoglycans.[4] A review describes stimulation of blood vessel and nerve outgrowth and anti-inflammatory actions.[2]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["A randomised controlled trial studied skin care with or without GHK-Cu after CO2 laser resurfacing.[6] Wikipedia reports several small, short controlled facial trials with anti-wrinkle results.[3] A 2025 review states wider evidence on permeability and effectiveness is insufficient.[1] A phase 2 trial of topical GHK-Cu gel for acute wounds is recruiting.[7]"],"subs":[]},{"h":"In peptide and looksmaxxing communities","paras":["looksmax.org has threads on GHK-Cu for skin, microneedling and acne, and on potency and reconstitution questions.[8][9] These are user discussions, not clinical guidance."],"subs":[]}],"lore":null,"timeline":[{"year":"1973","event":"Pickart isolates GHK-Cu from plasma.[3]"},{"year":"1977","event":"Identified as the tripeptide GHK.[3]"},{"year":"2006","event":"RCT of copper tripeptide after laser resurfacing.[6]"}],"refs":[{"title":"Topically applied GHK as an anti-wrinkle peptide","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39963574/","source":"BioImpacts","year":2025,"type":"review"},{"title":"Regenerative and Protective Actions of the GHK-Cu Peptide","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29986520/","source":"Int J Mol Sci","year":2018,"type":"review"},{"title":"Copper peptide GHK-Cu","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_peptide_GHK-Cu","source":"Wikipedia","year":2026,"type":"encyclopedia"},{"title":"Stimulation of collagen synthesis in fibroblast cultures by GHK-Cu","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3169264/","source":"FEBS Letters","year":1988,"type":"study"},{"title":"The potential of GHK as an anti-aging peptide","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35083444/","source":"Aging Pathobiol Ther","year":2020,"type":"review"},{"title":"Effects of topical copper tripeptide complex on CO2 laser-resurfaced skin","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16847171/","source":"Arch Facial Plast Surg","year":2006,"type":"trial"},{"title":"Topical GHK-Cu Gel for Acute Skin Wound Healing (NCT07437586)","